{:edb/db-before-t 4912 :edb/db-after-t 4913 :edb/tx-data [[13194139538225 50 #inst "2026-10-20T01:08:13.060Z" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186167544 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186167544 1074 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186171690 1074 "The period of $y = \\sin x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\sin{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= \\sin (x + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\sin (x + 6π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sin{(x+6\\pi)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\sin (x + 6π) - 3 &= -2(\\frac{1}{2}) - 3 \\\\\n&=-1 - 3 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186171690 1074 "The period of $y = \\sin x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\sin{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= \\sin (x + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\sin (x + 6π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sin{(x+6\\pi)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\sin (x + 6π) - 3 &= -2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) - 3 \\\\\n&=-1 - 3 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186171691 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sin{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sin{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\sin{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\sin(-x) = -\\sin{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x + 16π)|= \\sin (-x + 2π \\cdot 8); = \\sin (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x)|=-\\sin x; = - (-\\frac{4}{7}); = \\frac{4}{7}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x + 16\\pi) = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186171691 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sin{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sin{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\sin{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\sin(-x) = -\\sin{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x + 16π)|= \\sin (-x + 2π \\cdot 8); = \\sin (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x)|=-\\sin x; = - \\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right); = \\frac{4}{7}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x + 16\\pi) = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186171744 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186171744 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186171767 1074 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to be equal to $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_1}{\\sqrt{n_1}} = z_{0.01} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_2}{\\sqrt{n_2}}\n$$\n\nSolving for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}} \\\\\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}})^{2} &= (\\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}})^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01}^{2} \\cdot s_{2}^{2}}{z_{0.025}^{2} \\cdot s_{1}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\ns_1 = 5, s_2 = 3, z_{0.01} = 2.326, z_{0.25} = 1.960\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{2.326^{2} \\cdot 3^{2}}{1.960^{2} \\cdot 5^{2}} \\approx 0.507\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nn_1\\approx 1.97 n_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{smaller}}$ than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.97}$." 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186171767 1074 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to be equal to $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_1}{\\sqrt{n_1}} = z_{0.01} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_2}{\\sqrt{n_2}}\n$$\n\nSolving for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}} \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}}\\right)^{2} &= \\left(\\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01}^{2} \\cdot s_{2}^{2}}{z_{0.025}^{2} \\cdot s_{1}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\ns_1 = 5, s_2 = 3, z_{0.01} = 2.326, z_{0.25} = 1.960\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{2.326^{2} \\cdot 3^{2}}{1.960^{2} \\cdot 5^{2}} \\approx 0.507\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nn_1\\approx 1.97 n_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{smaller}}$ than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.97}$." 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186171809 1074 "To find $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{4} \\choose {\\color{blue}3}},$ we need to find the number that is located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important:** Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\fra