66b05919a7644818eea7265ff3ae8fc3ced4ac7d1d6d30352f612649393c876a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{13}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{13}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(13)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (13\\sqrt{13}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [175921861