& =-4{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-4({e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{15}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186183961 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+7x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4})+3 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186183961 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+7x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4})+3 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186184051 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $150^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $150^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)$. Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 150^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Now, let's consider the angle $240^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $240^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)$. Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 240^\\circ = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 240^{∘} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 240^{∘}} \\\\\n&= (1)/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} (150^{∘}) + 2\\csc^{2} (240^{∘}) &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} + 2(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + 2(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186184051 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $150^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $150^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)$. Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 150^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Now, let's consider the angle $240^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $240^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)$. Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 240^\\circ = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 240^{∘} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 240^{∘}} \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} (150^{∘}) + 2\\csc^{2} (240^{∘}) &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + 2\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186184052 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186184052 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186184053 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186184053 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186184054 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for t