{2} - (1)/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) \\\\ &= y^{2} - \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)^{2} = (y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186212849 1074 "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2} - 3^{2} \\\\ &= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3)(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186212849 1074 "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &= \\left(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2} - 3^{2} \\\\ &= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3)(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186212859 1074 "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186212859 1074 "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186212860 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{4}{3})^{3}(\\frac{4}{3})^{t} &= (\\frac{4}{3})^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186212860 1074 "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186212872 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nC(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the number of data packets processed, in thousands, $t$ minutes after the upgrade. We want to rewrite this model in terms of seconds rather than minutes.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{s}{60}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $60$ (not multiply) because we want $s=60$ seconds to correspond to $t=1$ minute.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $c(s)$ for the number of packets as a function of time in seconds $s$ after the upgrade:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(s) = C(\\frac{s}{60}) &= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2) \\cdot (s/60)} \\\\\n&= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{s/24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe base $1.05$ means that the number of packets grows by $5\\%$ each time the exponent increases by $1$, i.e., once every growth period.\n\nThe exponent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{24}\n$$\n\nshows that for every increase in $s=24$ seconds, the exponent increases by $1$.\n\nTherefore, the number of packets is expected to increase by $5\\%$ every ${24}$ seconds." 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186212872 1074 "We are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nC(t) = 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the number of data packets processed, in thousands, $t$ minutes after the upgrade. We want to rewrite this model in terms of seconds rather than minutes.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{s}{60}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $60$ (not multiply) because we want $s=60$ seconds to correspond to $t=1$ minute.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $c(s)$ for the number of packets as a function of time in seconds $s$ after the upgrade:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(s) = C\\left(\\frac{s}{60}\\right) &= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{(5/2) \\cdot (s/60)} \\\\\n&= 95 \\cdot (1.05)^{s/24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe base $1.05$ means that the number of packets grows by $5\\%$ each time the exponent increases by $1$, i.e., once every growth period.\n\nThe exponent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{24}\n$$\n\nshows that for every increase in $s=24$ seconds, the exponent increases by $1$.\n\nTherefore, the number of packets is expected to increase by $5\\%$ every ${24}$ seconds." 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186212879 1074 "Explanation:\n\nWe are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nP(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the population $t$ weeks after the chemical is introduced. We want to rewrite this model in terms of days instead of weeks.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $7$ days in a week, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{d}{7}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $7$ (not multiply) because we want $d=7$ days to correspond to $t=1$ week.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $p(d)$ for the population as a function of time in days $d$ after the chemical is introduced:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(d) = P(\\frac{d}{7}) &= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4) \\cdot (d/7)} \\\\\n&= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{3d/28}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage decrease over $2$ days. Inspecting the model in terms of days $p(d)$, we see that the decay factor over $d$ days is\n$(0.95)^{3d/28}$.\n\nSo, the decay factor over $2$ days is\n\n$$\n(0.95)^{3\\cdot 2/28} = (0.95)^{3/14} \\approx 0.989\\,069\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to a decrease of approximately\n\n$$\n1 - 0.989\\,069 = 0.010\\,931 \\approx 1.09\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the population is predicted to decrease by $1.09\\%$ every $2$ days." 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186212879 1074 "Explanation:\n\nWe are given the exponential model\n\n$$\nP(t) = 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the population $t$ weeks after the chemical is introduced. We want to rewrite this model in terms of days instead of weeks.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since there are $7$ days in a week, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{d}{7}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $7$ (not multiply) because we want $d=7$ days to correspond to $t=1$ week.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $p(d)$ for the population as a function of time in days $d$ after the chemical is introduced:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(d) = P\\left(\\frac{d}{7}\\right) &= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{(3/4) \\cdot (d/7)} \\\\\n&= 775 \\cdot (0.95)^{3d/28}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage decrease over $2$ days. Inspecting the model in terms of days $p(d)$, we see that the decay factor over $d$ days is\n$(0.95)^{3d/28}$.\n\nSo, the decay factor over $2$ days is\n\n$$\n(0.95)^{3\\cdot 2/28} = (0.95)^{3/14} \\approx 0.989\\,069\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to a decrease of approximately\n\n$$\n1 - 0.989\\,069 = 0.010\\,931 \\approx 1.09\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the population is predicted to decrea