t-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2p \\\\ y &=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{\\left(-\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186217794 1074 "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}(x\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186217794 1074 "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}(x\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186220844 1080 "\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{2})" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186220844 1080 "\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπx}{2}\\right)" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186220846 1080 "\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{2})" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186220846 1080 "\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπx}{2}\\right)" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186220863 1080 "\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{2})" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [1