all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{(n+1)!}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{n!}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{n!}{(n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{n!}{(n+1)\\cdot n!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{n!}}{(n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false whereas statement III is true. Since $0\\le L<1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233331 1071 "Determine the third partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233331 1071 "Determine the third partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}.$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233331 1074 "Let ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{r}^{3})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{[\\frac{3}{4}]}^{3})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{27}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{37}{64})}{(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{111}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233331 1074 "Let ${a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n