\n![](images/d5/d58ec3abe2b06d39ec4b8f66e785d433f6bfa6b8eb52faab98aae306a63debe5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233585 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233585 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233588 1071 "![](images/5e/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233588 1071 "![](images/5e/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{\\left(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\right)}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233588 1074 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233588 1074 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{\\left(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=4.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)=4(4)-12=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)={3}^{2}-2(3)+2=5\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}{\\left(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 3}(4f(x)-12)}{{lim}_{x\\to 3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233608 1071 "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=3.$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233608 1071 "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=3.$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233608 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233608 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233611 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x)(4x)-(\\operatorname{sec}x)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x)}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 false] [17592186233611 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x)(4x)-(\\operatorname{sec}x)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x)}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538225 17592186576411 true] [17592186233612 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}t+5\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}t-5\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cos}t-5\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}t-5\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-10\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-5\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-10{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}\